Pointer = A variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Benefit: They help avoid wasting memory by allowing you to pass the address of a large data structure instead of copying the entire data
* = dereference operator used to make pointers, accesses the actual value stored at the address held by the pointer.
& = gives you the memory address (hex) of a variable.
When there’s a pointer in a function name, you need to pass the address of whatever is needed.


#include <stdio.h>


void birthday(int *age);


int main() {


    int age = 25;
    int *pAge = &age;


    printf("%p\n", &age);
    printf("%p\n", pAge);


    birthday(pAge);
    printf("You are %d years old", age);
    return 0;
}


void birthday(int *age) {
    (*age)++;
}


int score = 95;
int *ptr = &score; // ptr now holds the memory address of score

printf("%d", *ptr); // Prints 95 (dereferencing)
*ptr = 100;         // score is now 100
